      "heading": "Start from the requirement, not the model",
      "narration": "This is the last lesson, and it is deliberately the one with the least new technology in it, because the thing that most often decides whether an ML project succeeds is not the model. It is whether anybody wrote down what the system was supposed to do. Every real brief you will receive is underspecified. Build us a recommender. Add AI to search. Make the support queue faster. Those are directions, not specifications, and the first piece of work is not modelling — it is turning that sentence into something with a measurable definition of done. Four questions, and the order matters. Who is this for, and what will they do differently because it exists? If nobody's behaviour changes, the system has no value regardless of its accuracy, and you will find this out after six months rather than after an afternoon. Second, what does success mean as a number? Not better search — click-through on the top three results, or reduction in queries that get abandoned. If you cannot name the metric you cannot tell whether you are finished. Third, what are the constraints? Latency, because a recommendation that takes four seconds is not a recommendation. Cost per request, because a system that works and cannot be afforded has not worked. Volume, because ten requests a day and ten thousand a second are different projects. Fourth, and this is the one people skip: what happens when it is wrong? Notice how much that changes. A wrong film recommendation costs the user a shrug, so you can be aggressive, ship fast and learn from behaviour. A wrong medication interaction alert costs a life, so you need review, explainability, an audit trail and a much higher confidence threshold before the system acts on its own. Same technology, completely different system, and the difference comes from that one question. The cost of an error is what determines how much confidence you need before acting, and it should be settled before anyone picks a model. And then the question that a surprising number of projects should answer differently: does this need machine learning at all? Rules are cheaper to build, faster to run, explainable by construction, testable, and they never drift. If the problem is genuinely covered by twenty rules, write twenty rules — you will ship in a week and everyone will understand the system. Machine learning earns its place when the rules would be too numerous to write down, or when they would change faster than you could maintain them, or when the pattern is real but nobody can articulate it. Choosing a model because a model was expected is the most expensive mistake in this lesson, and the version of it that shows up now is reaching for an LLM when a lookup table would do.",
