      "heading": "Baselines, and the rule that saves projects",
      "narration": "If you take one operational rule from this lesson, take this one: build the dumbest thing that could possibly work, first, before you build anything clever. For a recommender, that is recommending the most popular items to everybody. For search, returning the most recent matching document. For classification, always predicting the majority class, or a handful of keyword rules. It takes an afternoon. And it is the single highest-value afternoon in the project, for four separate reasons. The first is that it tells you whether the problem is hard. If popularity gets you eighty percent of the click-through of a sophisticated system, the problem was not hard, and everything you were planning to build was going to buy you the last twenty percent at a hundred times the cost. That is worth knowing before you plan a quarter around it, not after. The second is that it gives you the only number your later work can be judged against. Without a baseline, a model with seventy-eight percent accuracy is a meaningless statistic — it might be excellent or it might be worse than a coin weighted by class frequency. Every accuracy figure quoted without a baseline is uninterpretable, and this is worth remembering when you read other people's results too. The third is that the answer is frequently uncomfortable and always useful. It is genuinely common for the trivial baseline to reach eighty percent of the achievable gain for two percent of the effort. When that happens, the correct engineering response is to ship the baseline and go and work on something else. That feels like a defeat and it is a result — you found out cheaply that the sophisticated version was not worth building, which is exactly what the exercise was for. Projects that skip the baseline discover this at the end, after the investment, when nobody wants to hear it. And the fourth reason is the one people appreciate only in retrospect: the baseline proves the plumbing. To ship even the dumbest model you have to solve every problem in the system that is not the model. Getting the data out. Cleaning it. Computing a metric on a held-out set. Serving a prediction with acceptable latency. Logging what was served. Measuring the effect on the thing you actually care about. Every one of those is a place where real projects die, and every one of them is easier to fix now, with a trivial model behind it, than later when you are also debugging a transformer. The baseline is not a formality you perform before the real work. It is a load test of everything except the model, and it is the fastest way to find out which of your assumptions about the data were wrong.",
