      "heading": "Data, labels, and the loop that keeps it alive",
      "narration": "The model is the small part. The data pipeline is where projects actually succeed or fail, and there are four things to settle early. First, and it should be the very first question in any design discussion: where do the labels come from? This is more important than the architecture and it is asked far too late. Implicit labels come from behaviour you already collect — clicks, purchases, whether a support ticket was reopened, whether a suggestion was accepted. They are free and plentiful and they are biased, because they only exist for things the user was shown. Explicit labels come from someone deciding: an annotator, a moderator, a domain expert. They are clean, they are slow, and they cost real money. Most systems use both, and every system needs an answer here. A project that cannot say where its labels will come from is not a project yet. Second, split your data by time whenever time exists in it. Train on the earlier period, evaluate on the later one, because that is what production does — the model is always predicting the future from the past. A random split lets examples from next month sit in your training set, the model learns from a world it will not have access to, and your offline number is excellent and your production number is not. This one mistake accounts for an enormous share of models that looked good in the notebook and disappointed in deployment. Third, leakage, which is the same failure in a subtler costume. A feature that is computed after the outcome — an account status field updated when the customer churned. An identifier that happens to encode the answer because of how the records were created. The same user appearing in both training and test. Duplicated rows across the split. In every case the model learns something that will not be available at prediction time, and in every case the symptom is the same: a result that is better than you expected. Treat surprisingly good results as a bug report. Nearly always there is a leak, and finding it after you have promised the number to somebody is much worse than finding it now. Fourth, and this is the one that separates people who have run a system from people who have trained a model: design the feedback loop deliberately. Once deployed, your model changes the behaviour you are learning from. A recommender that shows an item causes the clicks that become tomorrow's training data, so the model reinforces its own choices and the catalogue narrows. A fraud model that blocks transactions never learns whether they were actually fraudulent. This is not avoidable, but it is manageable: log what was shown alongside what was chosen, so the two can be separated later. Reserve a small fraction of traffic for random or exploratory serving, so you keep learning about options the model has stopped suggesting. And monitor the input distribution, not only the accuracy, because the first sign of a decaying system is usually that the data changed rather than that the model got worse.",
