      "narration": "Watch how Iceburg takes a commission, because everything in this lesson is in it. He does not begin by choosing timber, and he does not begin by talking about construction techniques. He asks what the ship is for. What waters will she sail — the Grand Line is not the East Blue, and a hull built for one is wrong for the other. What is she carrying, and how much of it. How many crew, because that decides the accommodation and therefore the layout. How far between ports, which sets the stores and the water. Who will sail her, because a ship that needs forty trained hands is useless to a crew of nine. And the question he always asks and clients rarely expect: what happens if she is caught in the wrong weather? Notice that not one of those is a question about shipbuilding. They are questions about use, and every one of them constrains the design more tightly than any opinion about timber or rigging could. By the time he has the answers, most of the ship is already determined — the brief decided it, and the craft is what executes the decision. A shipwright who starts from I would like to build a fast ship, and works out what it is for afterwards, builds a beautiful vessel that is wrong for the job. That is exactly the project that starts from we should use a transformer. Then, before the yard commits any serious material, he builds a model of the hull and floats it. It is not the ship. It carries nothing, it has no rigging, and nobody would sail it. What it does is answer the only question worth answering at that stage, which is whether this shape works at all — whether it sits right in the water, whether it heels the way it should. If the model is wrong, he has lost a week. If he had found out from the finished hull, he would have lost the commission. That is the baseline, precisely: the cheapest possible object that tests the assumption everything else rests on, built specifically so that being wrong is affordable. And the last thing, which is the part of shipbuilding that people forget is part of shipbuilding. A ship is not finished at the launch. It is careened and scraped, planks are replaced, rigging is renewed, and it is refitted when it starts sailing different waters. Every shipwright knows the vessel's life is mostly maintenance, and the design accounts for it — you build so that the parts that will wear can be reached. Machine learning systems are the same and are frequently designed as if they were not. The data will drift, the users will change, the upstream feed will silently alter its format, and the model that was excellent in March will be quietly mediocre by September. If nobody owns that, the system is not permanent, it is just not yet obviously broken. A system nobody maintains was designed to be abandoned, and that is a decision somebody made — usually by not making it.",
