      "heading": "Deployment, monitoring, and how to answer this in an interview",
      "narration": "Two practical topics and then how to say all of this out loud, because you will be asked. Deployment first. Do not swap a system over. Start in shadow mode: run the new model on live traffic, log what it would have done, and act on nothing. This is remarkably informative and completely safe — you see the real input distribution, which is never the one in your test set, you find the crashes on inputs nobody thought of, and you can compare its decisions against what actually happened. Then release it to a small percentage of traffic behind a flag, with the ability to turn it off in seconds, and measure the metric you defined in the first slide. Expect the online result to be worse than the offline one. It nearly always is, because offline evaluation uses a fixed dataset and production has feedback, distribution shift and users who behave differently once the system exists. Offline metrics are a filter for what is worth testing online; they are not a prediction. Monitoring second, and the key insight is that you usually cannot monitor accuracy. Labels arrive days later, or only for the cases you acted on, or never. So monitor what you can see immediately: the distribution of inputs, the distribution of predictions, latency, cost per request, and error rates. A shift in the input distribution is the earliest warning you will get that something upstream changed, and it precedes the accuracy drop you cannot yet measure. Alert on the input distribution, and you find out that a data provider changed a field before your users find out that your product got worse. Now the interview, because this exact material is the ML system design round and there is a reliable way to do well in it. Spend the first five minutes on requirements, and do it visibly. Who uses this, what do they do differently, what is the metric, what is the scale, what is the latency budget, what does a wrong answer cost. Interviewers are specifically watching for whether you do this, and a candidate who names an architecture in the first minute has already lost marks that are hard to recover. Then walk the pipeline in order: baseline first, then data and labels, then features, then the model, then evaluation, then deployment, then monitoring, then failure modes. At each step, name a trade-off, say which side you chose, and say why — precision against recall for the metric, latency against accuracy for the model size, cost against freshness for retraining. The trade-offs are the substance. The right answer to almost every question in that round is a defensible choice with a stated reason, not a specific technology, and a candidate who says I would start with logistic regression to get a baseline and here is what would make me move past it is more convincing than one who names the largest model they know. And that is the course. You started with vectors on graph paper, you have derived backpropagation, built a transformer, aligned it, retrieved for it, given it tools, and now designed the system it lives in. The last thing worth saying is the thing this whole part has been circling: the model was never the hard part.",
